[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one bug, one fix queued. Its reports say otherwise.
They were merged on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
and the same subjects ("Date is wrong", "Month and day swapped?") came in for different bugs.
The queued parser fix closes one of them. The others stay open behind a ticket that says known.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser does not close #4471. Each problem below has its own ticket now.
</Callout>

## What the reports show

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

Counted from the export at render time: one report per id, placed by what its tenant runs, not by
its subject or its intake tag. Reports the desk read as a one-day move stay counted where they are
filed, as on the #4471 page, and count again as their own problem. Intake tags come from the reporter's browser; a tenant's people
often book from an en-US browser, so the tag is wrong in both directions.

### `parser`: the shared parser reads day-first dates month first

Every tenant writes the day first; the parser reads the month first. Day and month swap: "Our
7 April reminder went out on 4 July" (R-41200), "Booked for 2 December. The invite says
12 February" (R-41203). It comes in through the app, by email and through booking-v2 forms, from
every tenant. This is the fix that is already queued.

### `hint`: booking-v3 tells day-first tenants to type the month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. A day-first tenant's people type what the hint
says, and the form reads it the tenant's way. The result looks the same as the parser bug, a
swapped day and month, but only on booking-v3 forms (R-41241, R-41305, R-41387). The parser fix
does not touch it: the hint has to follow the tenant's locale. The desk's own table already put
these apart and marked them "no ticket of its own".

### `one-day`: bookings move by a single day

No swap. "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May" (R-41202). "Tuesday 6 October
showed as Monday" (R-41620). "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same
meeting" (R-41344). "The invite arrived before I made the booking" (R-41236). "Every booking in
June is one day off" (R-41209). That is a time zone or day-boundary bug, not date order. The desk
noted these reports (desk-notes.json) but counted them under `parser`, so the parser fix would
have been expected to close them.

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## What to change on the desk

- Merge on cause, not subject. The same words describe different bugs; [triage](/kb/triage/dedupe)
  can try `causeOf` before it merges. Decision 0001 stays as it is; a new record has to replace it.
- When a report is read as something else, it gets its own ticket, not a footnote under the old one.
- Close #4471 only when every ticket in the table above is closed.

